Obstructive sleep apnea
Opthalmology & ENT
Witnessed apneas/snoring, Excessive daytime sleepiness, Elevated BMI/crowded oropharynx

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: OET-027
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal waking with severe choking, gasping, and subsequent sustained confusion and hypertensive urgency.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an obese patient who awoke abruptly from sleep with a terrifying sensation of smothering, followed by profound confusion and a severe headache. Urgent physical assessment was strictly required to rule out acute nocturnal cardiac ischemia, transient ischemic attack (TIA), or severe acute hypoxemia, and to stabilize the acute hypertensive response prior to the morning.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I woke up feeling like someone was holding a pillow over my face, my heart was pounding out of my chest, and my head is splitting."
History of Present Illness: Symptoms peaked tonight when the patient woke up abruptly with a profound, terrifying sensation of choking and gasping. The spouse reports a long history of loud, highly disruptive, and chronic snoring continuously interrupted by witnessed respiratory pauses. The patient describes unrefreshing sleep resulting in distinct early morning frontal headaches, alongside unrelenting daytime sleepiness and chronic generalized fatigue despite 8 hours in bed. Endorses decreased daily cognitive performance and heightened daytime irritability.
Functional Status: Currently agitated, pacing the room, suffering from a severe frontal headache.
Social/Safety Concerns: Spouse is highly distressed, fearing the patient was dying during the apneic episode.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 185/105 | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 95% (Room Air) | Wt: 115 Kg
General: Obese, thick-necked, visibly fatigued but anxious, rubbing temples.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular, rapid rhythm with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally. Neurological exam reveals intact cranial nerves, normal speech, and no focal motor or sensory deficits. Airway assessment demonstrates a markedly elevated BMI, coupled tightly with a large neck circumference (45cm). The posterior oropharynx is severely crowded, featuring a low-hanging soft palate, a distinctly enlarged uvula, and massive tonsils (Mallampati Class IV).
Environmental Observations: Patient had been sleeping supine on a single flat pillow.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. On Perindopril for essential hypertension; compliance reported as good.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Obstructive Sleep Apnea - Status: Acute nocturnal exacerbation with hypertensive urgency
Differentials:

Acute Coronary Syndrome (Nocturnal Angina) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Normal ECG rhythm (assessed clinically), absence of crushing central chest pain, no diaphoresis, and clear respiratory etiology (witnessed apnea).

Transient Ischemic Attack (TIA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Completely normal neurological exam, normal speech, and the confusion resolving as the patient's oxygenation improved upon waking.

Nocturnal Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear chest auscultation without wheeze, and the mechanism being a clear upper airway mechanical collapse rather than lower airway bronchospasm.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Cardiovascular risk profile is high)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient experienced a severe, prolonged apneic event leading to acute nocturnal awakening, transient hypoxia-induced confusion, and a sympathetic surge resulting in hypertensive urgency. Urgent face-to-face evaluation was critical to differentiate this presentation from acute cardiopulmonary emergencies (ACS, TIA, pulmonary embolism). Clinical examination definitively linked the acute crisis to severe, undiagnosed obstructive sleep apnea based on the pathognomonic airway anatomy and spouse's witnessed history. By ruling out acute ischemic events, the visit prevented an unnecessary emergency department admission while immediately implementing positional airway management to prevent a recurrence tonight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered single dose of oral Paracetamol 1g for the hypoxia-induced headache. Observed until blood pressure trended downward (160/95) following return to calm wakefulness. Generated urgent referral for overnight polysomnography (sleep study).
Positive Behavioral Change: To immediately reduce the severity of the apneas tonight, the patient must sleep strictly in the lateral decubitus (side-lying) position, enforced by safety-pinning a tennis ball to the back of their pajama shirt. The physical discomfort encountered when attempting to roll onto the back immediately prompts the patient to return to their side (positive punishment of the supine position), mechanically preventing the tongue and soft palate from totally occluding the airway.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to expedite the sleep study and manage the secondary hypertension.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the patient develops crushing chest pain, pain radiating to the jaw or arm, sudden weakness on one side of the body, or difficulty speaking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Definitive diagnosis and severity grading require a formal overnight sleep study.
Clinical Course: The choking episodes will continue until treated. The severe headache and high blood pressure are direct results of your brain being starved of oxygen during sleep.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

If you develop any chest pain, shortness of breath while awake, or stroke-like symptoms, call 000 immediately, as your heart is under significant strain.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The heavy tissue in your neck relaxes and collapses when you sleep on your back, completely blocking your airway. You wake up choking because your brain panics from the lack of oxygen, which also spikes your blood pressure.
Actionable Steps: Implement the tennis-ball pajama trick immediately to force side-sleeping. Avoid all alcohol and sedatives before bed, as these further relax the airway muscles.

